# Untitled

High-Performance Inverted Polymer Solar Cells with Solution-Processed Titanium Chelate as Electron-Collecting Layer on ITO Electrode
Polymer solar cells (PSCs) have received considerable attention in recent years because of their potential application for low-cost solar energy conversion.[1,2] Since the bulk heterojunction (BHJ) PSC was reported in 1995,[3,4] power conversion efficiency (PCE) of the PSCs has been improved gradually by using new donor and acceptor photovoltaic materials and new device structures, and recently the PCE has reached $7-8\%.^{[5-8]}$ However, the PSCs are still far from competing with their inorganic counterparts with regard to efficiency and lifetime. Great efforts are needed to fur­ ther increase PCE and improve the working lifetime of the PSCs.
The conventional BHJ PSCs are composed of a photoactive layer sandwiched between an indium tin oxide (ITO)/poly(3,4- ethylenedioxythiophene): poly(styrenesulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS) positive electrode and a Ca (or LiF)/Al low-work-function nega­ tive electrode. However, both the PEDOT:PSS layer and the lowwork-function metal electrode are known to degrade the device lifetime,[9–11] since the low-work-function metal is susceptible to degradation by oxygen and water vapor, and the PEDOT:PSS layer is acidic and corrosive to the ITO electrode.
Fortunately, the work function of ITO electrode is tunable by the buffer layer on its surface. This establishes the founda­ tion of the inverted structure PSCs. Since the first report in 2006,[12,13] inverted PSCs have attracted growing attention in recent years.[14,15] In the inverted structure, the ITO electrode is modified by stable low-work-function $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ ,[16–18] $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ ,[19–22] or $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}[23-28]$ buffer layer, and it is acted as negative electrode. Meanwhile, the top electrode (positive electrode) is composed of PEDOT:PSS[18,19,21–24]or some high-work-function transition metal oxides $(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5},\mathrm{}^{[12,16,27]}\mathrm{~MoO}_{3},\mathrm{}^{[20,25,28]}\mathrm{~WO}_{3}\mathrm{}^{[26]})$ layer covered by a stable metal electrode, such as Au or Ag. Obviously, the inverted structure overcomes both problems with the interface instability of the ITO electrode caused by PEDOT:PSS and the instability of the low-work-function metal top electrode in the conventional PSCs.
A key component in the inverted PSCs is the electroncollecting buffer layer material on ITO electrode. Solutionprocessing and low temperature treatment of the buffer layer is desirable for low cost and large area fabrication of the PSCs, especially for flexible PSCs with plastic substrate. Although the $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ buffer layer can be prepared by annealing at a relatively lower temperature,[16,17] the buffer layer such as $\mathsf{Z n O}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ reported in literatures was prepared ether by vacuum-evaporation or with high temperature treatment (higher than $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) after solution-processing. Therefore, high efficiency solution-proc­ essed low-work-function buffer layer on ITO electrode still needs to be developed for high performance inverted PSCs.
In this study, we used an alcohol-soluble titanium chelate, titanium (diisopropoxide) bis(2,4-pentanedionate) (TIPD)[29] (see Figure 1a) as the electron collecting buffer layer on ITO as negative electrode in the inverted PSCs with $\mathsf{M o O}_{3}/\mathsf{A l}$ as positive electrode. The active layer of the PSCs is composed of the blend of poly(4,8-bis-alkyloxybenzo(1,2-b:4,5-b′)dithiophene-2,6-diyl-alt(alkyl thieno(3,4-b) thiophene-2-carboxylate)-2,6-diyl) (PBDTTT$\mathbf{C})^{[6,30]}$ (see Figure 1a) as donor and (6,6)-phenyl- ${\bf\cdot C}_{71}$ -butyric acid methyl ester $(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM})$ as electron acceptor. The buffer layer was prepared by spin-coating the TIPD isopropanol solu­ tion on ITO electrode and then thermal treated at $60{-}170~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10{-}30~\mathrm{min}$ . The power conversion efficiency of the inverted PSC with the TIPD buffer layer treated at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ reached $7.4\%$ under the illumination of AM1.5, $100\mathrm{\mW}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , which is increased by $16\%$ in comparison with that $(6.4\%)$ of the device with the conventional structure under the same experimental conditions. Moreover, the TIPD layer possesses the advantages of (1) solution processability with isopropanol solvent, which facili­ tates the solution-processing technologies such as spin-coating, doctor-blade and role-to-role printing; (2) lower annealing tem­ perature $(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , which is compatible with plastic substrates for flexible devices; (3) low cost since TIPD is a cheap and com­ monly used material in paints and inks to improve the adhesion with substrate. Moreover, the as-prepared TIPD layer is highly transparent for the visible light with an absorption peak in the UV region at ca. $340~\mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in Figure S1 in Supporting Information (SI). The UV absorption peak decreased signifi­ cantly after thermal treatment at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and was disappeared after thermal treatment at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (see Figure S1 in SI). The transpar­ ency in the visible region is also very important for the buffer layer on the transparent ITO electrode.
Figure 1b shows the device structure of the inverted PSCs, the photoactive area of the devices was defined by the intersec­ tion of the orthogonally aligned stripes of ITO and Al electrodes. Figure 1c displays the energy levels of the materials involved in the devices, where a-TIPD represents the TIPD annealed at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . The LUMO and HOMO energy level of a-TIPD were measured by electrochemical cyclic voltammetry (see Figure S2 in SI). The electronic energy levels of $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ ,[31] PBDTTT- $\cdot{\bf C}^{[30]}$ and $\mathsf{M o O}_{3}[25]$ were taken from the literatures. When light irradiates on the photoactive layer of the PSCs through ITO electrode, the active layer will absorb photons to produce excitons, and the excitons will diffuse towards and dissociate at the PBDTTT-C/ $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ interface into electrons in the LUMO (the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital) of the acceptor $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ and holes in the HOMO (the highest occupied molecular orbital) of the donor PBDTTT-C. Since the LUMO level $(-3.91~\mathrm{eV})$ of a-TIPD (the annealed TIPD) is the same as that of $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ , electrons can easily transport to ITO electrode through a-TIPD. While the HOMO level $(-6.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ of a-TIPD is greatly lower than that $(-5.12~\mathrm{eV})$ of PBDTTT-C, so that it blocks the hole transport from PBDTTT-C to the ITO electrode. On the other hand, the HOMO level $(-5.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ of $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ is very close to the HOMO level $(-5.12\ \mathrm{eV})$ of PBDTTT-C, which facili­ tates the hole transfer to Al electrode through $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ . While the relatively high-lying LUMO $(-2.3\mathrm{eV})$ of $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ prevents the elec­ tron transfer from $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ to the Al electrode. Thus, from the viewpoint of energy levels, the device could show high charge collection efficiencies on both electrodes.
Figure 2a compares the current density-voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the inverted PSC and the conventional PSC at dark and under the illumination of AM1.5G, $100\mathrm{\mw}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The inverted PSC is composed of $\mathrm{{ITO/a}}$ -TIPD $(12\ \mathrm{nm})$ )/PBDTTT- $\mathrm{\cdotC}{:}\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ $(80\ \mathrm{nm})/\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ $(24~\mathrm{nm})/\mathrm{Al}$ $[100\mathrm{nm})$ where a-TIPD buffer layer was transformed from TIPD layer through thermal annealing at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . And the conventional PSC is in the structure of ITO/PEDOT:PSS $(30\mathrm{\nm})/\mathrm{PBDTTT-C:PC_{71}B M}$ $(80\ \mathrm{\nm})/\mathrm{Ca}$ (10 nm)/Al $(100\ \mathrm{nm})$ ). The device performance parameters (average of 16 devices), obtained from the $J.$ –V curves, are summarized in the inset of Figure 2a. The inverted PSC shows a PCE of $7.4\%$ with a short-circuit current density $(J_{s c})$ of $16.26~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , an open-circuit voltage $(V_{o c})$ of $0.70{\mathrm{V}},$ and a fill factor (FF) of 0.65. Based on 16 devices with the same device structure, PCE of the PSCs is $7.4(\pm0.3)\%$ , which is among the highest values in the inverted PSCs reported so far. In contrast, the control conventional device shows a PCE of $6.4\%$ (average of 16 devices) with a $J_{s c}$ of $14.63~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , a $V_{o c}$ of $0.70{\mathrm{V}},$ and a FF of 0.63. These values of the conventional PSC are consistent with those reported in Ref. [30]. Compared with the conventional PSC, the increased $J_{s c}$ of the inverted device could be ascribed to enhanced light reflection from back electrode and the spa­ tially redistributing of the light intensity inside the device due to $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ and a-TIPD modification layers in the device.[32,33] The slightly increased FF of the inverted device could result from the decrease in series resistance from $2.64\ \Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for the con­ ventional device to $1.03\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for the inverted device.
We compared the incident photon to converted current efficiency (IPCE) spectra of the inverted and the conventional devices together with the absorption spectra of PBDTTT-C, $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ and their blend films, as shown in Figure 2b. For the absorbance measurements, the $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ film was prepared by spin-coating a $18.75~\mathrm{mg/mL~PC_{71}B M}$ dichlorobenzene solution at $1500~\mathrm{rmp}$ on quartz slide, PBDTTT-C film was obtained by spin-coating a $12.5~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ PBDTTT-C dichlorobenzene solu­ tion at $1500\ \mathrm{rpm}$ on quartz slide, and their blend film was formed by spin-coating a blend dichlorobenzene solution of $12.5~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ PBDTTT-C and $18.75~\mathrm{mg/mL~PC_{71}B M}$ on quartz slide. The maximum IPCE value of the inverted device is $69\%$ in comparison with $61\%$ for the conventional device. The IPCE results agree with the higher $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the inverted PSC men­ tioned above. The higher IPCE of the inverted PSC could also be ascribed to enhanced light reflection from back electrode and the spatially redistributing of the light intensity inside the inverted device, as mentioned above.
Effect of thermal annealing of the TIPD buffer layer on the photovoltaic performance of the inverted PSCs was studied by changing the annealing temperature from 60 to $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Figure 3 and Figure S3(a) in SI shows $J{-}V$ curves of the inverted PSCs, and their photovoltaic parameters (average of 16 devices) were summarized in Table 1. The IPCE spectra of the corresponding devices are shown in Figure S3(b) in SI. For the device with the TIPD layer without thermal annealing, the PCE is only $3.7\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $9.67\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . After the TIPD layer was annealed at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ , $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and PCE increased to $14.57~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and $5.1\%$ respectively. Further increasing the annealing tempera­ ture of the TIPD buffer layer improved the photovoltaic per­ formance further, and the best thermal annealing condition is at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ where $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and PCE reached $\bar{16.26\ m A/\mathrm{cm}^{2}}$ and $7.4\%$ respectively. Here, we should mention that $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is a common temperature for the thermal annealing treatment of PEDOT:PSS layer and the active layers in high performance PSCs, and it is endurable for the plastic substrates used in future flexible PSCs.
In order to investigate the mechanism of the effect of the thermal annealing of the TIPD layer on the photovoltaic per­ formance of the devices, we carried out detailed characteriza­ tions of the TIPD samples with different thermal annealing treatments. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the TIPD films, as shown in Figure S4 in SI, indicate that the as-prepared TIPD film and the a-TIPD film after thermal annealing at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ and at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ all are in amorphous state. The electron mobility of the TIPD layers, measured with spacecharge-limited current (SCLC) method as shown in Figure S5 in SI, is ca. $8.6\times10^{-5}$ , $2.8\times10^{-4}$ and $1.9\times10^{-3}\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{V}\cdot\mathrm{s}$ for the TIPD without annealing, annealed at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respec­ tively. The increased electron mobility after thermal annealing should be beneficial to charge transportation to the electrode.
The surface contact angle of the TIPD layer before and after thermal treatment, as shown in Figure S6 in SI, reflects the change of surface properties. Without thermal annealing, the surface contact angle of the TIPD layer is $24^{\circ}$ , while it changed to $60^{\circ}$ after annealed at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , which indicates that the surface of TIPD layer changed from hydrophilic to hydro­ phobic after the thermal annealing. Further increasing the
