The chemical change of TIPD films before and after thermal annealing was analyzed by Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) spectra as shown in Figure 4. It can be seen that the IR peaks of the as-prepared TIPD at 1124, 1161, 2864, 2927, and $2968~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ disappeared and a new peak at $1026~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appeared after thermal annealing at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ , indicating some component change of TIPD molecule occurred during the thermal annealing. The IR peaks at 1124 and $1161~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to isopropoxy group in TIPD,[34,35] and those at 2864, 2927, and $2968~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are the absorptions of C-H stretching to the stoichiometric ratio of 1:1.67 in TIPD. The C 1s peak positions of a-TIPD sample are the same with those of TIPD. But the atomic ratio of C 1s $(286.4~\mathrm{eV})$ and C 1s $(284.8~\mathrm{eV})$ peaks changed to 1:1.57, which is close to the stoichiometric ratio of 1:1.50 in TOPD. The XPS results further confirm the structural change of TIPD to TOPD after the thermal treatment at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ .
